# CODEOWNERS — route review of CI/CD and security-critical surfaces to an owner.
#
# `.github/` (workflows / Actions / Dependabot) is the highest-value target for
# supply-chain attacks ("poisoned pipeline execution"): one PR that rewrites a
# workflow can exfiltrate secrets / tokens.
#
# This file ROUTES review to an owner. Whether anything BLOCKS a merge is a
# property of the ruleset, not of this file. Read it; do not infer it here:
#   gh api repos/<owner>/<repo>/rulesets --jq '.[].id'
#   gh api repos/<owner>/<repo>/rulesets/<id> --jq '{rules, bypass_actors}'
# Measured 2026-09-03 01:10 JST: require_code_owner_review true, but
# required_approving_review_count 0 and bypass_actors grants RepositoryRole 5
# bypass_mode "always" -- so ownership here routes review and blocks nothing.
# An earlier version of this comment said it "mechanically blocks" once branch
# protection enabled code-owner review. That precondition is now satisfied and
# the claim still did not come true, so the hedge was falsified, not unmet.
#
# Syntax: <pattern> <owner...>  — last match wins (most specific line applies).

# CI / GitHub Actions / Dependabot automation.
/.github/                @theosera

# CODEOWNERS itself (self-defense against tampering).
/.github/CODEOWNERS      @theosera

# Agent guardrails + security policy — weakening these weakens the whole posture.
/SECURITY.md             @theosera
/CLAUDE.md               @theosera
/CLAUDE.global.md        @theosera
/.claude/                @theosera

# Security boundary code: path containment + frontmatter field allowlist.
/src/pathSafety.ts       @theosera
/src/frontmatter.ts      @theosera
